WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.972 --> 00:00:02.760
There are an estimated...

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:07.150
this many individual viruses on Earth.

00:00:07.150 --> 00:00:09.200
Luckily, there are only a little over

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:13.480
1,000 virus strains
known to infect humans,

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:17.101
the majority of which
come from other animals.

00:00:17.101 --> 00:00:19.840
They're part of a group
called zoonotic diseases,

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:24.580
caused by bacteria, viruses,
parasites, or fungi.

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:26.740
And the one we're all focusing on now

00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:29.610
is the virus that causes COVID-19.

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:32.870
And new ones can emerge at any time.

00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:34.080
Here's what needs to happen

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:38.080
for a virus to jump
from animals to humans.

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:40.064
As a pathogen, the virus' goal

00:00:40.064 --> 00:00:42.396
is to infect its host and replicate,

00:00:42.396 --> 00:00:44.371
because it can't do that on its own.

00:00:44.371 --> 00:00:47.660
Let's say this pig is the
original host of a virus.

00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:50.260
He and his buddies form a reservoir,

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:53.210
a specific population of
animals of the same species

00:00:53.210 --> 00:00:55.720
that naturally host a pathogen.

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:57.614
Colin Parrish: So, there are
millions of viruses out there

00:00:57.614 --> 00:00:59.957
infecting animals. Literally millions.

00:00:59.957 --> 00:01:02.165
I mean, the more we
look, the more we find.

00:01:02.165 --> 00:01:04.306
And most of those viruses

00:01:04.306 --> 00:01:06.660
don't infect, you know, other animals.

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:08.550
They have a restricted host range.

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:10.020
Narrator: Most of the time, the virus

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:12.000
doesn't affect its original host

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:14.290
or only mildly affects it.

00:01:14.290 --> 00:01:17.100
So, what's it doing in there?

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:19.280
Suppose that this pig hosts a virus

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:21.790
that primarily infects the gut.

00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:24.190
Viruses tend to attack
different parts of the body,

00:01:24.190 --> 00:01:27.390
depending on whether they
can bind to these guys.

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:31.000
Receptors are proteins found
along the outside of a cell,

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:33.780
used to communicate with
the rest of the body.

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:36.230
But viruses can latch on, too.

00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:39.380
Cells in a respiratory system
may have different receptors

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:41.927
than cells in a digestive tract.

00:01:41.927 --> 00:01:45.160
A virus does its thing by
latching onto a host cell,

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:48.050
entering it or injecting
bits of itself into it,

00:01:48.050 --> 00:01:49.830
and then hijacking it.

00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:52.970
It forces the cell to
make copies of the virus,

00:01:52.970 --> 00:01:56.670
all of which will go on to
hijack other host cells.

00:01:56.670 --> 00:01:59.210
This will usually kill the host cell.

00:01:59.210 --> 00:02:01.730
And if enough of the
host cells are infected

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:03.610
and make more of the virus,

00:02:03.610 --> 00:02:05.560
the host will contract an infection,

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:09.270
which, if the body can't fight
it off or fights too hard,

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:12.340
could lead to severe disease or death.

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:15.740
In reservoirs, however, the
species has likely evolved

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:19.700
a resistance to the virus
over many generations.

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:22.210
This allows a sort of equilibrium;

00:02:22.210 --> 00:02:24.280
the immune system controls the infection

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:26.850
without killing the virus off completely.

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:28.730
If the virus jumps, though,

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:32.440
a new host won't have that
same, or any, immunity.

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:34.740
That might sound scary, because truthfully

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:38.200
you are constantly being
exposed to viruses.

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:40.162
But only a very small number

00:02:40.162 --> 00:02:42.970
succeed at infecting a new host species.

00:02:42.970 --> 00:02:44.686
It's called spillover,

00:02:44.686 --> 00:02:46.644
and there are a series of barriers

00:02:46.644 --> 00:02:50.230
that a virus must navigate
for that to happen.

00:02:50.230 --> 00:02:52.630
If it's held up by even one of these,

00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:54.650
it can be stopped in its tracks.

00:02:54.650 --> 00:02:57.620
Simplified, they represent two things:

00:02:57.620 --> 00:03:00.010
Can the virus get to the new host's cells,

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:02.350
and can it bind and enter them?

00:03:02.350 --> 00:03:04.530
The more infected pigs
there are in one space

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:06.400
and the closer they are to people,

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:08.910
generally the more chance of spillover.

00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:10.780
But the likelihood also has to do with

00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:13.590
how the humans are interacting with them.

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:16.050
Animal viruses are usually
transmitted to people

00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:19.070
in a few ways: contact with excretions,

00:03:19.070 --> 00:03:22.130
slaughter, bites, contact with tissues,

00:03:22.130 --> 00:03:23.888
or through an intermediate species

00:03:23.888 --> 00:03:25.990
like mosquitoes or ticks.

00:03:25.990 --> 00:03:28.787
So places like farms and slaughterhouses

00:03:28.787 --> 00:03:30.210
and even petting zoos,

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:32.480
where people come in close
contact with animals,

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:34.960
have an increased risk of spillover.

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:37.420
Proximity alone isn't enough, though.

00:03:37.420 --> 00:03:39.970
Some of it can be genetic for humans.

00:03:39.970 --> 00:03:42.650
There's a huge list of genes
that have been associated

00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:44.973
with different risks of infections,

00:03:44.973 --> 00:03:47.440
some genes offering resistance
to certain infections

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:49.740
and others increasing risk.

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:51.760
Beyond genes, the virus has to get through

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:54.343
the body's innate immune responses.

00:03:54.343 --> 00:03:56.313
Parrish: So, there's two
types of immune responses.

00:03:56.313 --> 00:03:59.260
One is the simple
adaptive immune responses,

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:02.297
so those are antibodies and T cells,

00:04:02.297 --> 00:04:04.410
and they generally get stimulated

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:06.980
after the infection has already occurred.

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:09.780
The innate responses are the
ones that are already present

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:13.533
inside the cell that
make a cell or a host,

00:04:13.533 --> 00:04:15.980
you know, resistant to the virus.

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:17.740
Narrator: Unlike an adaptive response,

00:04:17.740 --> 00:04:20.740
an innate one can attempt
to fight off any pathogen

00:04:20.740 --> 00:04:22.670
rather than a specific one.

00:04:22.670 --> 00:04:26.610
Mucus membranes, stomach
acids, skin, sentinel cells,

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:29.240
and even just a lack
of the right receptors

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:31.830
can stop a virus from infecting a person.

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:35.200
So, this is when mutations
are really important.

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:37.420
A successful spillover
usually doesn't happen

00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:38.969
with the original virus.

00:04:38.969 --> 00:04:41.040
Parrish: They have to gain some mutations

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:44.050
that allow them to
replicate most efficiently,

00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:46.443
allow them to overcome
those host barriers.

00:04:46.443 --> 00:04:47.540
Narrator: A virus that infects

00:04:47.540 --> 00:04:49.300
the digestive system of the pig

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:51.760
might attack respiratory cells in humans.

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:54.450
It depends on what receptor
the virus is suited for

00:04:54.450 --> 00:04:56.660
or mutates to be suited for.

00:04:56.660 --> 00:04:58.550
Once inside the new host cell,

00:04:58.550 --> 00:05:00.616
an infection will only be successful

00:05:00.616 --> 00:05:02.960
if the virus can replicate.

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:06.080
Typically, infected cells
will release interferons,

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:08.520
proteins that stop the
virus from replicating

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:11.100
within the cell and in nearby cells,

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:12.990
which contain the infection and stop it

00:05:12.990 --> 00:05:15.030
from spreading to new cells.

00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:16.271
If that doesn't work,

00:05:16.271 --> 00:05:18.621
the adaptive immune system kicks in.

00:05:18.621 --> 00:05:22.040
Your T cells suss out and
kill already infected cells

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:24.290
to stop them from making more new virus,

00:05:24.290 --> 00:05:26.810
while your white blood
cells pump out antibodies

00:05:26.810 --> 00:05:30.200
specifically tailored to
fight this new pathogen.

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:33.150
But because the body has
never seen this virus before,

00:05:33.150 --> 00:05:35.670
it can take weeks to
produce the right ones.

00:05:35.670 --> 00:05:39.260
And immunodeficiencies in
either type of response

00:05:39.260 --> 00:05:42.160
can make it even easier
for a virus to take hold.

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:45.300
So, if a virus gets through all that,

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:48.410
contact, infection, replication,

00:05:48.410 --> 00:05:51.030
then it has successfully spilled over.

00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:51.863
But...

00:05:51.863 --> 00:05:53.570
Parrish: The virus has
to be able to transmit.

00:05:53.570 --> 00:05:57.590
It has to be able to be shipped
from that original person,

00:05:57.590 --> 00:05:59.810
and it has to be able to infect, you know,

00:05:59.810 --> 00:06:02.850
at least one or two more additional people

00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:05.650
so that you can start a
chain of transmission.

00:06:05.650 --> 00:06:07.450
Narrator: A virus infecting two people

00:06:07.450 --> 00:06:11.020
has double the odds of going
on to infect additional people,

00:06:11.020 --> 00:06:13.620
compared to a virus infecting just one.

00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:15.640
And this can continue until it reaches

00:06:15.640 --> 00:06:18.730
epidemic and pandemic proportions.

00:06:18.730 --> 00:06:22.360
COVID-19 was certainly not
the first zoonotic disease,

00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:23.970
and it won't be the last.

00:06:23.970 --> 00:06:26.860
Viruses don't want to kill their hosts;

00:06:26.860 --> 00:06:29.010
no host means no virus.

00:06:29.010 --> 00:06:31.164
But new diseases are so dangerous

00:06:31.164 --> 00:06:33.421
because humans don't
have the same immunity

00:06:33.421 --> 00:06:35.720
as the virus' reservoir host.

00:06:35.720 --> 00:06:37.745
And because there are so many,

00:06:37.745 --> 00:06:39.987
it's currently not possible to predict

00:06:39.987 --> 00:06:43.170
when or what specific
viruses will spill over,

00:06:43.170 --> 00:06:46.350
but we do know the conditions
in which spillover can occur.

00:06:46.350 --> 00:06:48.640
Parrish: You know, how
and where they might occur

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:51.387
and how we can put in place sort of

00:06:51.387 --> 00:06:54.090
better monitoring so that
we can catch them early

00:06:54.090 --> 00:06:56.030
and, as they say, stamp them out

00:06:56.030 --> 00:06:58.122
before they get to the
point where they become

00:06:58.122 --> 00:07:00.291
sort of an out-of-control epidemic.

